def register(T):
    # ===== SCIENCE =====

    T["the Coriolis effect"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "an apparent deflection of moving objects observed in a rotating reference frame, most familiar as the rightward deflection of winds and currents in the Northern Hemisphere and leftward in the Southern Hemisphere caused by Earth's rotation",
        "how": "in an inertial frame, an object in motion travels in a straight line, but the ground beneath it rotates with the Earth. From the ground's perspective, the object appears to curve. The deflection magnitude is proportional to the object's speed, the sine of latitude, and twice the planet's angular velocity, with no deflection at the equator and maximum at the poles",
        "why": "the effect shapes large-scale atmospheric and oceanic circulation, sets the rotation direction of cyclones and hurricanes, drives the trade winds, and constrains long-range artillery and missile trajectories. Meteorology and oceanography are unintelligible without it",
        "vs": "unlike a real force such as gravity, the Coriolis force is a fictitious or inertial force that exists only in the rotating frame of an observer; from space it disappears. It also differs from the centrifugal force, which acts radially outward, while Coriolis acts perpendicular to the velocity vector",
        "ex": "a hurricane in the Atlantic spins counterclockwise because air flowing inward toward the low-pressure center is deflected to the right of its motion. Long-range artillery shells fired north in mid-latitudes drift several hundred meters east due to Coriolis deflection over their flight time",
        "mis": "people believe the Coriolis effect determines which way water spirals down a bathroom sink. At that scale the effect is utterly swamped by basin shape, residual swirl from filling, and tap geometry. Coriolis only dominates over horizontal scales of hundreds of kilometers and time scales of hours or longer",
    }

    T["quantum tunneling"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "a quantum-mechanical phenomenon in which a particle passes through a potential energy barrier that, by classical mechanics, it lacks the energy to surmount. The particle's wavefunction extends into and through the barrier with exponentially decaying amplitude",
        "how": "in the Schrodinger equation, the wavefunction inside a barrier higher than the particle's energy decays exponentially rather than vanishing. If the barrier is thin enough, a nonzero amplitude leaks through to the other side, where the wavefunction resumes oscillating. The transmission probability falls exponentially with barrier width and the square root of the height-energy gap",
        "why": "tunneling drives nuclear fusion in stars (protons would otherwise never overcome Coulomb repulsion), powers alpha decay, enables the scanning tunneling microscope to image individual atoms, and underlies flash-memory programming and tunnel diodes. Without it, the Sun would not shine",
        "vs": "tunneling differs from classical barrier crossing where energy must equal or exceed the barrier height. It differs from quantum reflection (a particle bouncing off a barrier despite having more than enough energy) in that tunneling penetrates a barrier that classically should reflect it. It also differs from over-the-top thermal activation, which dominates at high temperatures",
        "ex": "in a scanning tunneling microscope, a sharp metal tip held about a nanometer above a conducting surface drives a tiny tunneling current across the vacuum gap. The current depends so sensitively on distance (changing by a factor of ten per angstrom) that scanning the tip across the surface maps individual atoms with sub-angstrom resolution",
        "mis": "people think tunneling lets particles teleport, but the wavefunction is continuous on both sides and the particle's probability density inside the barrier is real, not zero. Another myth is that tunneling violates energy conservation; energy is conserved on average, with the brief barrier traversal allowed by the time-energy uncertainty relation",
    }

    T["Bose-Einstein condensates"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "a state of matter formed when bosons (particles with integer spin) are cooled to within a billionth of a degree of absolute zero, at which point a macroscopic fraction of them collapses into the lowest available quantum state and behaves as a single coherent quantum entity",
        "how": "as temperature drops, the de Broglie wavelength of each atom grows until it overlaps with neighbors. Below a critical temperature, identical bosons preferentially occupy the ground state because Bose-Einstein statistics favor multiple particles in the same state. Laser cooling and evaporative cooling in magnetic traps reach the required temperatures, typically 100 nanokelvin",
        "why": "BECs let physicists probe quantum mechanics at human scales: the condensate has a single wavefunction kilometers wide compared to atomic dimensions. They enable atom interferometers of extreme precision, model superfluidity and superconductivity, simulate condensed-matter Hamiltonians, and test fundamental physics like CPT invariance",
        "vs": "BECs differ from ordinary gases (random thermal motion) and from solids (frozen lattice positions) by being macroscopically quantum coherent. They differ from superconductors, where Cooper pairs of electrons condense, in that BECs involve neutral atoms and lack the charged-current properties. They differ from fermionic condensates of paired fermions like helium-3",
        "ex": "the 1995 BEC of rubidium-87 atoms by Cornell, Wieman, and Ketterle (Nobel Prize 2001) cooled about 2000 atoms below 170 nanokelvin in a magnetic trap. Time-of-flight imaging showed a sharp central peak rising above the thermal cloud, the unmistakable signature of macroscopic ground-state occupation",
        "mis": "people think a BEC is just a very cold gas, but it is a genuinely new phase characterized by macroscopic phase coherence and superfluidity. Another myth is that BECs require special exotic atoms; almost any boson works (rubidium, sodium, hydrogen, lithium-7), and even photons in dye-filled microcavities have been condensed",
    }

    T["the uncertainty principle"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "a fundamental quantum-mechanical limit on how precisely certain pairs of complementary observables can be simultaneously known. Heisenberg's relation states that the product of position and momentum uncertainties is at least h-bar over two, with similar relations for energy-time and other conjugate pairs",
        "how": "the principle follows mathematically from non-commuting operators in quantum theory: if two observables are represented by operators that do not commute, their values cannot be simultaneously definite in any quantum state. The position and momentum operators have a commutator equal to i*h-bar, which yields the uncertainty product directly via the Robertson-Schrodinger inequality",
        "why": "the principle constrains every measurement at quantum scales, sets the zero-point energy of bound systems, stabilizes atoms against electron collapse into nuclei, governs the resolution of microscopes and particle accelerators, and underlies the Casimir effect and vacuum fluctuations in quantum field theory",
        "vs": "the uncertainty principle differs from measurement disturbance in classical physics, which is in principle reducible by gentler measurement, in that no measurement protocol can ever beat the quantum bound. It also differs from statistical uncertainty over an ensemble; even individual quantum states have built-in spread",
        "ex": "an electron confined to a region the size of an atomic nucleus would have a momentum uncertainty so large that its kinetic energy would exceed the binding energy of any nucleus, which is why electrons orbit nuclei rather than being captured by them. Likewise, the energy-time relation lets virtual particles fluctuate from the vacuum on timescales h-bar over their energy",
        "mis": "people think the uncertainty principle is about the observer disturbing the system. While Heisenberg's original microscope argument used disturbance heuristics, the modern formulation is a property of the wavefunction itself, present even when no measurement is made. Another myth is that it applies to all observable pairs; only non-commuting (conjugate) pairs are constrained",
    }

    T["chirality in molecules"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "the geometric property of a molecule that makes it non-superimposable on its mirror image, like a left and right hand. Chiral molecules exist as pairs of enantiomers, often centered on a carbon atom bonded to four different groups, with identical scalar properties but opposite optical rotation",
        "how": "a chiral center, typically a tetrahedral carbon with four distinct substituents, creates two arrangements that cannot be rotated into one another. R and S designations come from the Cahn-Ingold-Prelog priority rules. Chemists separate enantiomers via chiral chromatography, asymmetric synthesis with chiral catalysts, or selective crystallization with a chiral resolving agent",
        "why": "biology is profoundly chiral: proteins use only L-amino acids, DNA only D-sugars, and enzymes are exquisitely sensitive to handedness. Drug efficacy and toxicity often differ dramatically between enantiomers, which is why the FDA increasingly requires single-enantiomer formulations and why chiral synthesis is a multi-billion-dollar industry",
        "vs": "chirality differs from cis-trans isomerism, which involves spatial arrangement around a double bond rather than mirror-image relationships. It differs from constitutional isomerism (different connectivity) and from conformational variation (interconvertible by rotation). Two enantiomers have identical melting points, NMR spectra, and most reactions; only chiral environments distinguish them",
        "ex": "carvone has two enantiomers: (R)-carvone smells like spearmint, while (S)-carvone smells like caraway, because olfactory receptors in the nose are themselves chiral. Thalidomide's tragic 1960s history is the canonical pharmaceutical example: one enantiomer treated morning sickness, the other caused severe birth defects",
        "mis": "people think enantiomers behave identically in all contexts; they only behave identically in achiral environments. In a chiral environment (every biological system, every chiral solvent, every polarized-light measurement), enantiomers can behave wildly differently. Another myth is that chiral synthesis is exotic; it is now routine pharmaceutical practice",
    }

    T["polymer chemistry"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "the study of macromolecules built from many repeating monomer units linked by covalent bonds. Polymers include synthetic plastics (polyethylene, nylon, polystyrene), elastomers (rubber), fibers (polyester, Kevlar), and biological macromolecules (proteins, DNA, cellulose)",
        "how": "monomers join through addition polymerization (free radicals propagating across double bonds, as in polyethylene) or condensation polymerization (loss of small molecules like water during bond formation, as in nylon). Chain length, branching, cross-linking, and tacticity determine bulk properties. Catalysts like Ziegler-Natta enable controlled stereochemistry and chain-length distributions",
        "why": "polymers underpin modern material life: packaging, clothing, electronics, vehicles, medical devices, and adhesives are mostly polymeric. Tailoring chain architecture lets chemists tune density, strength, transparency, conductivity, and biodegradability. Without synthetic polymers, no plastic bottles, no airbags, no contact lenses, no MRI gradients",
        "vs": "polymers differ from small molecules in that they are statistical mixtures of chain lengths rather than discrete species, so properties depend on distributions (number-average vs weight-average molecular weight). Thermoplastics (reversibly meltable) differ from thermosets (cross-linked, infusible). Linear vs branched vs cross-linked architectures yield very different mechanical responses",
        "ex": "ultra-high-molecular-weight polyethylene (UHMWPE) used in artificial hip joints has chains of millions of carbons each, giving extreme abrasion resistance. Kevlar, an aromatic polyamide with rigid rod-like chains aligned by liquid-crystalline processing, is so strong by weight that bullet-resistant vests use only a few millimeters of woven fabric",
        "mis": "people think all plastics are interchangeable, but a single bond in a monomer (carbon-carbon vs carbon-oxygen, all-carbon vs aromatic) reshapes performance entirely. Another myth is that biodegradable always means good; many bioplastics need industrial composting at controlled temperature and humidity to break down, not ordinary landfill conditions",
    }

    T["molecular orbital theory"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "a quantum-mechanical model of bonding in which atomic orbitals on neighboring atoms combine to form molecular orbitals that span the whole molecule. Electrons fill these molecular orbitals according to the Aufbau principle, the Pauli exclusion principle, and Hund's rule, giving rise to bonding, antibonding, and nonbonding states",
        "how": "linear combinations of atomic orbitals (LCAO) yield molecular orbitals: in-phase combinations produce bonding orbitals lower in energy than the parent atomic orbitals, out-of-phase combinations produce antibonding orbitals higher in energy. Symmetry constrains which atomic orbitals can mix. Filling order then determines bond order, magnetism, and ionization energies",
        "why": "MO theory explains phenomena that simple Lewis structures cannot: oxygen's paramagnetism, the bond order of fractional values, the colors of organic dyes, the conductivity of conjugated polymers, and the electronic structure of metal complexes. It is the foundation of computational chemistry and quantum-chemical methods like DFT and Hartree-Fock",
        "vs": "MO theory differs from valence bond theory, which views bonding as overlapping localized atomic orbitals between specific pairs of atoms. MO is delocalized; VB is localized. For ground-state energies, modern MO methods (post-Hartree-Fock, DFT) are usually more accurate, while VB language remains intuitive for chemists drawing structures and predicting reactivity",
        "ex": "molecular oxygen has a bond order of two but two unpaired electrons in its degenerate antibonding pi-star orbitals, making it paramagnetic, a fact MO theory predicts directly but Lewis dot structures cannot. Liquid oxygen is famously sticky to a magnet because of this paramagnetism",
        "mis": "people think MO theory and VB theory contradict each other; they are equivalent when carried to convergence, just expressed in different basis sets. Another myth is that MOs are physical objects; they are mathematical solutions of a one-electron approximation, useful for thinking but not directly observable",
    }

    T["the citric acid cycle"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "also called the Krebs cycle or TCA cycle, a series of eight enzymatic reactions in the mitochondrial matrix that oxidizes the acetyl group of acetyl-CoA to two carbon dioxide molecules, capturing the energy as NADH, FADH2, and GTP. It is the central hub of aerobic metabolism for carbohydrates, fats, and amino acids",
        "how": "acetyl-CoA condenses with oxaloacetate to form citrate, which is rearranged to isocitrate, oxidatively decarboxylated to alpha-ketoglutarate (releasing CO2 and NADH), further oxidized and decarboxylated to succinyl-CoA (more CO2 and NADH), then converted through succinate, fumarate, malate, and back to oxaloacetate, generating GTP, FADH2, and another NADH along the way",
        "why": "the cycle is the metabolic crossroads of cellular life. Its NADH and FADH2 feed the electron transport chain, producing roughly two-thirds of the ATP your cells make. Its intermediates also serve as precursors for amino acids, heme, and fatty acid synthesis, so the cycle runs in both catabolic and biosynthetic directions",
        "vs": "the cycle differs from glycolysis (cytoplasmic, anaerobic-capable, produces pyruvate and a small amount of ATP) by being mitochondrial, strictly aerobic in net flux, and producing the bulk of reducing equivalents. It differs from the urea cycle (nitrogen disposal) and the pentose phosphate pathway (NADPH and ribose generation) in central role",
        "ex": "during sustained aerobic exercise, your skeletal muscle mitochondria run the cycle at high flux: pyruvate from glucose enters as acetyl-CoA, fatty acids from beta-oxidation enter as acetyl-CoA, and oxaloacetate is regenerated cycle after cycle. Citrate transported out of mitochondria becomes the carbon source for fatty acid synthesis when energy is plentiful",
        "mis": "people think the cycle directly produces large amounts of ATP, but each turn yields only one GTP. The huge ATP output comes downstream when NADH and FADH2 donate electrons to the electron transport chain. Another myth is that it runs only in the catabolic direction; many intermediates flow outward as biosynthetic precursors continually",
    }

    T["mitosis vs meiosis"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "two distinct types of eukaryotic cell division. Mitosis produces two genetically identical diploid daughter cells from one parent and is responsible for growth and tissue repair. Meiosis produces four genetically distinct haploid daughter cells from one diploid parent and is responsible for forming gametes (sperm and eggs)",
        "how": "mitosis is one round of DNA replication followed by one division: prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase, separating sister chromatids. Meiosis is one round of replication followed by two divisions. Meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes (with crossing-over recombination during prophase I) and is reductional; meiosis II separates sister chromatids like mitosis",
        "why": "mitosis lets multicellular organisms grow from one cell to trillions and replace lost tissue without genetic drift. Meiosis generates genetic variation through independent assortment and recombination, which is the raw material of natural selection, and halves chromosome count so fertilization restores diploidy without doubling each generation",
        "vs": "mitosis preserves chromosome number and genome identity; meiosis halves the number and reshuffles the genome. Mitosis has one division producing two cells; meiosis has two divisions producing four. Mitosis happens in nearly every dividing somatic cell; meiosis happens only in germ-line cells. Crossing-over occurs in meiosis I, never in mitosis under normal conditions",
        "ex": "a fertilized human egg with 46 chromosomes divides by mitosis trillions of times to build the body, every cell genetically identical (barring mutation). When that body matures, germ-line cells in the gonads undergo meiosis: a primary spermatocyte with 46 chromosomes produces four sperm with 23 each, every sperm genetically distinct because of recombination",
        "mis": "people think meiosis is just a fancier mitosis; in fact, the mechanics are deeply different in meiosis I where homologous chromosomes pair, recombine, and segregate together. Another myth is that recombination is rare; on average each human chromosome experiences one or two crossovers per meiosis, so every gamete is genetically unique",
    }

    T["genetic mutation types"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "permanent changes in DNA sequence, classified by scale and effect. Point mutations alter single bases (substitutions split into transitions and transversions); indels insert or delete bases; large-scale changes include duplications, inversions, translocations, and copy-number variants. By coding effect: silent, missense, nonsense, frameshift",
        "how": "mutations arise from DNA-replication errors, spontaneous deamination of cytosine to uracil, oxidative damage producing 8-oxoguanine, UV-induced thymine dimers, ionizing radiation breaking strands, and chemical mutagens forming adducts. Repair systems (mismatch repair, base excision, nucleotide excision, double-strand break repair) catch most lesions, but escapes accumulate as inheritable mutations",
        "why": "mutations are the raw material of evolution and the origin of inherited disease and cancer. Single-base changes can convert a normal gene to an oncogene, abolish enzyme activity in a metabolic disorder, or shift binding affinity of a protein. Conversely, beneficial mutations underlie adaptation, antibiotic resistance, and species divergence",
        "vs": "silent mutations leave the encoded amino acid unchanged because of codon redundancy. Missense substitutes one amino acid for another with variable functional impact. Nonsense introduces a premature stop codon, usually truncating the protein. Frameshift indels (not multiples of three) shift every downstream codon and almost always destroy function. Large structural variants reshape entire chromosomes",
        "ex": "sickle cell anemia is a single missense mutation: a single A-to-T substitution in the beta-globin gene changes glutamate to valine at position six, causing hemoglobin to polymerize when deoxygenated and deforming red blood cells. Cystic fibrosis is most often a three-base deletion (delta F508) that removes one amino acid from the CFTR chloride channel",
        "mis": "people think all mutations are harmful, but the vast majority are neutral (in noncoding DNA or causing silent codon changes), and a small fraction are beneficial. Another myth is that mutations only happen in germ cells; somatic mutations accumulate continuously in body cells throughout life and drive cancer and aging",
    }

    T["the immune response"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "the coordinated reaction of the body's defensive cells and molecules to detect, contain, and eliminate pathogens or abnormal cells. It has two arms: innate immunity (rapid, generic, pattern-based) and adaptive immunity (slower, specific, memory-forming, mediated by B and T lymphocytes)",
        "how": "innate cells like macrophages and neutrophils recognize pathogen-associated patterns via receptors such as Toll-like receptors and respond within minutes by phagocytosis and cytokine release. Dendritic cells present antigens to T cells in lymph nodes, activating helper and cytotoxic T-cell clones. B cells produce antibodies that neutralize, opsonize, or fix complement onto pathogens. Memory cells persist for years to decades",
        "why": "without immune defense, even minor infections become lethal, as seen in severe combined immunodeficiency. With a properly tuned immune system, you survive thousands of microbial encounters per day. Vaccines exploit memory formation to prepare immunity before exposure; cancer immunotherapy weaponizes T cells against tumors; transplant medicine fights against the system's tendency to reject foreign tissue",
        "vs": "innate immunity is pattern-based and immediate; adaptive immunity is antigen-specific and develops over days. Humoral immunity (antibodies, B cells) handles extracellular pathogens; cellular immunity (cytotoxic T cells) handles intracellular ones. Tolerance keeps the system from attacking self; allergy and autoimmunity arise when tolerance fails. Active immunity follows infection or vaccination; passive immunity comes pre-formed (maternal antibodies, immunoglobulin therapy)",
        "ex": "after a flu vaccine, dendritic cells take up the inactivated viral antigens, migrate to lymph nodes, and present peptides to naive T cells. Helper T cells activate B cells specific to the antigens, which mature into plasma cells secreting neutralizing antibodies and into memory cells. Months later, when live virus arrives, memory B and T cells respond within hours instead of days",
        "mis": "people think a stronger immune response is always better; in fact overactive immunity drives cytokine storms, autoimmunity, and severe allergic reactions. Another myth is that boosting immunity with supplements is straightforward; nutrient deficiencies impair immunity, but extra micronutrients beyond sufficiency rarely help and can harm",
    }

