    T["matrices and determinants"] = {
        "_cat": "math",
        "what": "a matrix is a rectangular array of numbers arranged in rows and columns that represents a linear transformation between vector spaces. The determinant is a scalar function of square matrices that captures how the transformation scales volumes and whether it preserves or reverses orientation",
        "how": "matrices are added componentwise and multiplied row-by-column with the standard rule. Determinants are computed by cofactor expansion, row reduction with sign tracking, or specialized formulas (2x2: ad - bc; 3x3: rule of Sarrus). For large matrices, LU decomposition gives the determinant as the product of pivots in O(n^3) time. Geometrically, abs(det A) is the volume of the image of the unit cube under A",
        "why": "matrices encode linear transformations cleanly, letting computers handle rotations, projections, and least-squares fits with bulk operations. Determinants detect singularity (det = 0 means non-invertible), compute volumes in change-of-variable formulas for integration, and express cross products and orientations in geometry",
        "vs": "matrices contrast with scalars (single numbers) and vectors (1D arrays) as 2D arrays. Square matrices have determinants; rectangular ones do not. Determinants differ from traces (sum of diagonal): both are invariants but capture different information. The determinant is also distinct from the rank (number of linearly independent rows or columns)",
        "ex": "a 2D rotation by angle theta has matrix [[cos, -sin], [sin, cos]] with determinant 1, confirming it preserves areas and orientation. A reflection across the x-axis has matrix [[1, 0], [0, -1]] with determinant -1, indicating it preserves area but flips orientation. A scaling by factor k along both axes has determinant k^2",
        "mis": "people think matrix multiplication is commutative; it is not in general, and the order matters fundamentally for transformations. Another myth is that the determinant has no geometric meaning; it is precisely the signed volume scaling factor of the transformation. A third confusion is that a zero determinant just means small; it means the transformation collapses some dimension entirely",
    }

